# ubuntu-uki-iso kernel trim
# =====================
#
# Applied on top of /boot/config-<ver>-generic by ubuntu_uki_iso.build.kernel, then
# resolved with `make olddefconfig`. Disables drivers for hardware that is not
# present on ubuntu-uki-iso.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A LINE
# ------------------------------
# This fragment is the *only* thing allowed to remove things. Anything that
# must survive goes in never-disable.list, which ubuntu_uki_iso.build.kernel checks
# after the merge and fails the build over. Adding an `is not set` line here
# for something on that list breaks the build loudly rather than silently
# producing a kernel that cannot run containers, or whose test suite reports a
# machine with no disks.
#
# The list is not only about Docker. If this project needs a driver at
# runtime, for any reason, it belongs there.
#
# Symbol names below were taken from the real config-7.0.0-31-generic, not from
# memory. A symbol that does not exist in the base config is a no-op that
# merge_config.sh warns about but tolerates, so a typo here fails open: run
# `ubuntu-uki-iso doctor` after editing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build hygiene — not a driver trim, but these break the build or bloat it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Namespace the uname release, e.g. 7.0.0-ubuntu-uki-iso. This is what the UKI
# filename and the `uname -r` verification in the README key off.
CONFIG_LOCALVERSION="-ubuntu-uki-iso"

# The Ubuntu source tree references Canonical's module-signing certificates.
# A standalone bindeb-pkg build has no access to that PKI, and the build fails
# late (well after the expensive compile) if these point at absent files.
# Empty means "sign with nothing", which is what an unsigned, Secure-Boot-off
# image wants anyway.
CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYS=""
CONFIG_SYSTEM_REVOCATION_KEYS=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wireless
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_WLAN is the gate for the whole wireless stack: setting it off removes
# every vendor driver beneath it. CFG80211/MAC80211 are separate symbols and
# are not selected by it.

# CONFIG_WLAN is not set
# CONFIG_CFG80211 is not set
# CONFIG_MAC80211 is not set
# CONFIG_RFKILL is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bluetooth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_BT gates every BT_* sub-option.

# CONFIG_BT is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sound
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_SOUND gates the entire SND_* tree.

# CONFIG_SOUND is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Legacy buses and dead radio
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CONFIG_HAMRADIO is not set
# CONFIG_ISDN is not set
# CONFIG_MISDN is not set
# CONFIG_PCCARD is not set
# CONFIG_PCMCIA is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ethernet — keep Intel, drop everything else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NET_VENDOR_* are menu gates; one line removes a whole vendor's driver set.
# NET_VENDOR_INTEL is deliberately kept: igc (the I226-V) lives there, and
# e1000/e1000e come along with it, which is what makes the QEMU test harness
# work without a separate kernel.

